
Lab 2: Automated CI/CD Pipeline Optimization using Jenkins and MLflow
Objective
Set up a local CI/CD pipeline using Jenkins and use MLflow to log, analyze, and optimize build and deployment times using historical run data.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine
Install Jenkins:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openjdk-11-jdk jenkins -y
sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
Install MLflow and required Python packages:
pip install mlflow pandas matplotlib
Access Jenkins via browser:
http://localhost:8080

Initial admin password:

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Steps
Step 1: Create a Jenkins CI/CD Job
Create a Freestyle project called MLflow_CI_CD_Job.

In Build Steps, add the following shell script:

#!/bin/bash

START=$(date +%s)

# Simulate build and deployment
echo "Running build simulation..."
sleep $((RANDOM % 5 + 2))  # simulate build time (2–6 sec)

echo "Running deployment simulation..."
sleep $((RANDOM % 4 + 2))  # simulate deploy time (2–5 sec)

END=$(date +%s)
DURATION=$((END - START))

# Log data to CSV
mkdir -p ~/mlflow-ci-logs
echo "$(date),$DURATION" >> ~/mlflow-ci-logs/build_times.csv
echo "Build+Deploy Time: $DURATION seconds"
Save and Build the project multiple times to generate a history of runtimes.
Step 2: Track Performance with MLflow
Create a Python script log_to_mlflow.py in ~/mlflow-ci-logs/:
import pandas as pd
import mlflow
import os

mlflow.set_tracking_uri("file://" + os.path.abspath("mlruns"))
mlflow.set_experiment("CI_CD_Optimization")

df = pd.read_csv("build_times.csv", names=["timestamp", "duration"])

with mlflow.start_run():
    avg_time = df["duration"].mean()
    latest_time = df["duration"].iloc[-1]

    mlflow.log_metric("latest_build_time", latest_time)
    mlflow.log_metric("average_build_time", avg_time)
    print(f"Logged to MLflow: latest={latest_time}, average={avg_time}")
Run this script after several Jenkins builds:
cd ~/mlflow-ci-logs
python3 log_to_mlflow.py
Step 3: Launch MLflow UI
mlflow ui --port 5000
Access MLflow tracking UI at:
http://localhost:5000

You can now view historical build durations, trends, and optimization metrics.

Optional: Add MLflow to Jenkins Job
Append the following to your Jenkins job shell step:
python3 ~/mlflow-ci-logs/log_to_mlflow.py
This allows every Jenkins run to automatically log metrics to MLflow.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Created a local CI/CD pipeline using Jenkins

Logged and tracked build/deploy durations across multiple runs

Integrated MLflow to monitor and visualize performance trends

Enabled data-driven decisions to optimize pipeline efficiency — all offline

This lab introduces machine learning observability into CI/CD and demonstrates how performance telemetry can help automate DevOps optimization.
